            <ArticleTitle>QTc interval and electrocardiographic changes by type of shift work.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1174-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/ajim.22207</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">We conducted a cross sectional survey of electrocardiographic changes among shift-workers.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We classified the electrocardiogram morphology, and measured the QTc interval in 91 male workers engaged in regular daily work-shifts, 32 in 24 hr work-shift (h24), and 93 in irregular 6 hr work-shift (h6).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">With reference to daily workers, the QTc interval was prolonged among h6 workers (P &lt; 0.001) and h24 workers (P &lt; 0.005). The age- and obesity-adjusted standardized prevalence ratio (SPR) of a borderline/prolonged QTc was 2.2-fold among h6 workers (95% CI 1.2, 4.2); conduction disorders (SPR = 2.6; 95% CI 1.3, 5.2) and repolarization disorders (SPR = 1.9; 95% CI 1.0, 3.5) were also more frequently observed among h6 workers. Excluding 19 subjects with risk factors for prolonged QTc did not change the results.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Our study reveals significant changes in the ECG morphology in relation to shift-work, especially in unpredictable and non-standard working hours.</AbstractText>
